<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Nizari Ismaili state</h1><p class="st-meta">1090 CE – 1256 CE · capital Alamut</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The network of mountain castles in Iran and Syria ruled by the Nizari Ismailis, famous in legend as the 'Assassins', that defied the Seljuks for over 150 years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-i-sabbah">Hasan-i Sabbah</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> missionary, seized the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> in 1090. When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> passed over the heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizar-ibn-al-mustansir">Nizar</a> in 1094, Hasan and his followers supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar's</a> line, breaking with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and founding an independent Nizari movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state was a scattered collection of fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudbar-of-alamut">Rudbar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quhistan">Quhistan</a> and, from the 12th century, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-sinan">Rashid al-Din Sinan</a> became known to the crusaders as the 'Old Man of the Mountain'. Unable to match Seljuk armies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizaris</a> used targeted killings by devotees (fida'is) against hostile leaders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> had a famous library; the scholar Nasir al-Din al-Tusi lived among them. In 1164 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ii-of-alamut">Hasan II</a> proclaimed the qiyama, the spiritual resurrection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu's</a> Mongol army forced the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-din-khurshah">Rukn al-Din Khurshah</a>, to surrender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in 1256; he was later killed. The Syrian castles fell to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> by 1273. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aga-khan">Aga Khans</a> trace their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> to this line.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nizari-ismaili-state.jpg" alt="Nizari Ismaili state" width="320" height="274"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alamut.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1090 CE – 1256 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alamut</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hasan-i Sabbah, Hasan II, Rukn al-Din Khurshah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Nizari Ismaili Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alamut state, Nizari state, Assassins</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1173&amp;focus=nizari-ismaili-state" aria-label="Nizari Ismaili state on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5952 863 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nizari Ismaili state lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6272 1119L5921 1165M6272 1119L7559 780"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5921 1165h0M7559 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6272 1119h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6272 1119h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1173&amp;focus=nizari-ismaili-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

